WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.615
There are three cards on the wall.

00:00:03.615 --> 00:00:05.164
Add a reminder.

00:00:05.164 --> 00:00:07.230
Rewrite the first task.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.296
Give staff a dashboard.

00:00:09.296 --> 00:00:14.977
Every card has someone who can make a case for it.

00:00:14.977 --> 00:00:26.338
The meeting could become a contest of confidence, and the team could leave with a list that feels balanced but teaches little.

00:00:26.338 --> 00:00:32.019
Put the feedback ledger from the last lesson beside the cards.

00:00:32.019 --> 00:00:42.864
F zero three, F zero four, and F zero five suggest that the first task may need clearer direction and purpose.

00:00:42.864 --> 00:00:48.545
F zero one suggests another need: confirmation that an action worked.

00:00:48.545 --> 00:00:52.676
None of those six notes proves a solution.

00:00:52.676 --> 00:00:58.873
They do give us a reason to ask a better roadmap question.

00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:05.587
Chapter Eight says to turn features into bets about customer and business outcomes.

00:01:05.587 --> 00:01:13.333
A bet states what we believe will change, for whom, and how we will know.

00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:31.925
For the membership service, write: “If a clearer first-task card helps new members understand the next action, more of them should begin that task without staff translating the instruction.” That is a hypothesis, not a promise.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:36.056
The other two cards stay on the wall.

00:01:36.056 --> 00:01:44.319
We need a reason to choose now and a signal that would bring an alternative back.

00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:50.000
The roadmap should carry both, in words the team can remember.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:53.128
Place four questions under each card.

00:01:53.128 --> 00:01:56.777
Does the idea address the observed problem?

00:01:56.777 --> 00:02:01.991
Does it fit the outcome we are trying to improve?

00:02:01.991 --> 00:02:06.161
Can we test it at a responsible cost?

00:02:06.161 --> 00:02:09.289
What do we still not know?

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:17.109
The first-task guide has a plausible connection to our ledger and a small test path.

00:02:17.109 --> 00:02:27.536
A reminder may help people return, but our six notes do not yet show that forgetting is the main barrier.

00:02:27.536 --> 00:02:36.919
A staff dashboard could improve support work; we would need staff evidence before making that the lead bet.

00:02:36.919 --> 00:02:41.611
These are judgments in the example, not measured rankings.

00:02:41.611 --> 00:02:47.867
We are not assigning made-up scores to make the choice look scientific.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:54.645
Chapter Eight's prioritization warning is that an ordered list can hide weak reasoning.

00:02:54.645 --> 00:03:02.986
It asks what we say no to and what signal would make us revisit that answer.

00:03:02.986 --> 00:03:07.156
Write the first-task guide as the current bet.

00:03:07.156 --> 00:03:30.095
Next to the reminder, write “revisit if observations show people understand the task but forget to return.” Next to the dashboard, write “revisit if staff cannot see where members need help.” The Alignment plate can orient us to the next part of the book.

00:03:30.095 --> 00:03:33.223
It does not prove this choice.

00:03:33.223 --> 00:03:40.000
The evidence ledger and the criteria carry that burden, and invite careful review.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.171
Now the bet enters a product requirements document.

00:03:44.171 --> 00:03:50.427
A blank template can make a team start with controls and screens.

00:03:50.427 --> 00:04:05.545
For this exercise, begin one line earlier: “A new member opens the first task and cannot tell what action matters or why.” Under that line, link the ledger IDs.

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:10.237
Name the affected situation from the living persona card.

00:04:10.237 --> 00:04:21.185
State the desired behavior: the member can identify the next action and begin it without asking staff to translate the instruction.

00:04:21.185 --> 00:04:25.877
This is the comprehension layer Chapter Nine asks for.

00:04:25.877 --> 00:04:33.697
A developer should be able to explain why the work exists before describing the interface.

00:04:33.697 --> 00:04:36.303
Then add the proposed scope.

00:04:36.303 --> 00:04:46.209
We will test one first-task card with a clear action, a short reason, and a visible confirmation after beginning.

00:04:46.209 --> 00:04:56.114
We will not build a full reminder system, redesign the whole journey, or claim that this card increases retention.

00:04:56.114 --> 00:04:59.242
Those exclusions protect the learning question.

00:04:59.242 --> 00:05:06.019
They also leave room for engineering and design to find a better implementation.

00:05:06.019 --> 00:05:13.318
The document is a map of the problem, evidence, intended behavior, limits, and measure.

00:05:13.318 --> 00:05:17.488
It is not a script for every click.

00:05:17.488 --> 00:05:30.000
If the team spots a safer or simpler way to learn, the PRD should make that conversation possible before the build grows too large.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.746
Before anyone builds, ask a teammate to explain the bet back in their own words.

00:05:37.746 --> 00:05:44.977
“We want to see whether a clearer first task helps a new member begin.

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:51.690
We are testing one card, and we will observe the starting moment.” Good.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:57.887
Then ask what happens if the member presses begin and loses connection.

00:05:57.887 --> 00:06:01.502
Does the interface say the action worked?

00:06:01.502 --> 00:06:04.601
Can the person safely try again?

00:06:04.601 --> 00:06:09.765
F zero one already warned us that confirmation may matter.

00:06:09.765 --> 00:06:18.028
A document that says “show success” without defining this case leaves a real decision to chance.

00:06:18.028 --> 00:06:23.709
Chapter Nine recommends pre-testing the handoff for ambiguity and edge cases.

00:06:23.709 --> 00:06:33.521
A model may suggest questions from material your team can safely use, but its answer is not engineering sign-off.

00:06:33.521 --> 00:06:40.751
A person who will build or support the change needs to review the meaning.

00:06:40.751 --> 00:06:55.211
Add an acceptance check: after a successful start, the member sees a clear state; after a failed attempt, the card says what happened and what to do next.

00:06:55.211 --> 00:06:59.343
The team may refine that check during design.

00:06:59.343 --> 00:07:02.958
Record who made the decision and why.

00:07:02.958 --> 00:07:16.901
This is a small handoff, yet it contains the same failure mode as a large program: the source question can disappear while the document becomes more detailed.

00:07:16.901 --> 00:07:20.000
Keep it visible at the top.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:31.362
Imagine the team tests the card and finds that people understand the action but still cannot tell whether they have finished it.

00:07:31.362 --> 00:07:34.460
Imagine that result for a moment.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:40.657
The next move may be better completion feedback rather than more instruction.

00:07:40.657 --> 00:07:44.272
Update the PRD and the trace card.

00:07:44.272 --> 00:07:56.150
Keep the original assumption, add the new observation with its source and method, and record the date, owner, and reason for the change.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:08:08.545
Chapter Nine's living document is how decisions made in review, design, and development remain visible to the people who were not in every conversation.

00:08:08.545 --> 00:08:13.192
The roadmap should change with that learning as well.

00:08:13.192 --> 00:08:27.653
It is a story about the outcome we are trying to move and the bets we are willing to test, not a schedule that erases its own history.

00:08:27.653 --> 00:08:32.300
Our trace card has a source: the L05 ledger.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:36.948
It has a current decision: test the first-task guide.

00:08:36.948 --> 00:08:41.080
It has an owner and a limited scope.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.146
It names the exclusions.

00:08:43.146 --> 00:08:56.056
The measure is whether a participant can identify and begin the intended action in a planned test, with the observation method stated before the result.

00:08:56.056 --> 00:08:59.155
We have not measured it yet.

00:08:59.155 --> 00:09:10.000
A clean trace lets a future teammate ask why this work entered the plan and see an answer they can challenge.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:14.605
Pause and draw one line across your decision file.

00:09:14.605 --> 00:09:19.209
Start with the source IDs that support the problem.

00:09:19.209 --> 00:09:25.349
State the roadmap bet in a sentence a teammate could disagree with.

00:09:25.349 --> 00:09:34.558
Name the person or team who owns the decision, the smallest useful scope, and what is outside it.

00:09:34.558 --> 00:09:39.674
Write one acceptance check that a builder can explain back.

00:09:39.674 --> 00:09:49.907
Then choose a measure that would tell you whether the customer behavior changed, and say how you will observe it.

00:09:49.907 --> 00:09:58.093
Resume when each item points to the next without requiring someone to guess what you meant.

00:09:58.093 --> 00:10:05.767
If you are using a real project, keep identifiable records in your team’s research system.

00:10:05.767 --> 00:10:13.442
The line helps us follow the handoff from the roadmap bet to the living PRD.

00:10:13.442 --> 00:10:20.605
If a review uncovers a different problem, change the card with a dated reason.

00:10:20.605 --> 00:10:25.721
Do not quietly swap the measure after seeing a result.

00:10:25.721 --> 00:10:30.837
The next lesson will put learning checkpoints beside delivery work.

00:10:30.837 --> 00:10:34.419
For now, the trace is the safeguard.

00:10:34.419 --> 00:10:47.209
It lets the team build with enough freedom to solve the problem while preserving the question that brought the work here for the next review.

00:10:47.209 --> 00:10:54.372
Ask a teammate to point to the original feedback note and the success check.

00:10:54.372 --> 00:11:00.000
If they cannot, the line still has a gap to repair.
